title: The forest in the store
tags: Storage
kind: reference
audience: dev
summary: Each tree_ref node keeps the bitmap of its descendants and of its children, so a subtree is one read and a move costs the depth, not the size.

For every `tree_ref` field, each node with children keeps the bitmap of ''all'' its
descendants, and each target the bitmap of the ids pointing at it (a folder's
children, a `ref`'s referrers). A node holds one position per forest
(spec-data-model "One position per forest"), which is what makes this set
arithmetic:

* ''a node is created'' under a parent: its id joins each ancestor's bitmap — about depth chunk writes;
* ''a subtree moves'': its descendants and itself leave each old ancestor's bitmap and join each new one's — about depth operations, ''independent of the subtree's size'' (a move of 750 000 records took 6 ms, measured);
* ''a rename'' changes no bitmap: the node's forest key moves.

What it buys:

* `field ->* "/dir"` — a subtree — is one bitmap read, as a tag subtree is (`tag ->* (path =>* "music")`).
* A folder filter is the children bitmap: a key per 65 536 children.
* ''Sorting by path'' below a bound (a folder, a subtree) walks the forest in key order — each folder's children read a page at a time, ordered by name bytes — and stops at the page's end, resuming a cursor by descending its key's components. It declines, and the page is fetched and sorted instead, on a name that is not plain UTF-8 above the separator or is keyed by hash, or without a bound.
* An order-sensitive `osm` path ([[Text search in the store]]) finds its candidate ''anchors'' by trigrams, verifies each on its assembled path (its ancestors read once, shared between candidates), and takes the union of the verified anchors' subtrees. When an earlier term's rarest trigram names fewer folders than half the anchors, the anchors are first cut to those folders' subtrees (`dir4321 file` on 100 000 records: 210 008 keys read → 75).
* A `:path` comparison seeks instead of walking: `field:path = "<p>"` goes down `p`'s names, one read per level; a pattern anchored on a literal prefix (`^/music/j`) seeks the folder of the prefix's last separator; an ordering enters only the subtrees that can fall on the right side. An unanchored pattern (`mp3$`) still walks the whole forest.
* The forest invariants (no cycle, depth ≤ 1000) need only the ancestor chain: about depth reads per checked write.

The price is about depth times more chunks per tree write. Opening a folder — the
GUI's central gesture — costs a handful of reads in return.

`tests/kv_cost.rs` pins it: a page of a 16 000-entry folder sorted by path, a subtree
by path, and a count over a subtree of 800 folders read what they read on an eighth
of it.
